{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DNURTqZ5kaqmgCGpLnGNAC","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# researchPrime â€” Can Foreman run its build efforts in parallel to cut wall-clock?\n2\t\n3\t> Research session on grass idea **grass-4199 \"Improved Foreman (make parallel)\"** (Skill Foundry).\n4\t> Goal: reduce wall-clock to a Foreman deliverable WITHOUT reducing structural integrity, effectiveness, or robustness.\n5\t\n6\t## STAMPS (honesty)\n7\t- **Mode:** ENGINE-capable (Node import probe GO; 28 trio symbols crossed). Phase-3 governed loop run via **real fresh-context sub-agents** applying the engine's governance rules (heterogeneous reviewers, â‰¥2-agree for a blocker, a separate context-free Judge, convergence-until-dry) â€” NOT the Node engine's in-process scoring path.\n8\t- **ISOLATION:** real (parallel-swarm host; each reviewer a genuinely separate model context).\n9\t- **cross_model: false** â€” single model substrate. Correlated-blind-spot caveat: the 3 reviewers share a model family. Mitigation: core findings are grounded in OBSERVED Foreman/Crucible source AND independently corroborated by the separate prior study `researchPrime/runs/foreman-review-2026-06-19/FINAL-REPORT.md`.\n10\t- **Evidence ladder:** OBSERVED (code) > CORROBORATED > CLAIMED > UNVERIFIED.\n11\t\n12\t## BOTTOM LINE\n13\tParallelism is **mostly the wrong lever** for the stated goal. The \"don't parallelize code-writing\" rule is correct; the boundary is sharper than the one-line rule states. Two independent reasons make write-parallelism lose (it is \"over-determined\"):\n14\t\n15\t1. **Conflict-safety.** Parallel writers conflict on **semantic interface contracts**, not just files. In real plans nearly every wave threads a shared record field / seed builder through ~4 files at once; a single sequential author IS the guarantee. A file-diff \"disjointness\" check is blind to the semantic collision.\n16\t2. **Quota topology (decisive).** Every model step â€” coder OR reviewer â€” draws from ONE Pro/Max subscription window (~1â€“3 waves/window; no live quota API). N parallel model agents drain the one window NÃ— faster, then stall. So `wall_clock_floor â‰ˆ total_tokens / window_rate`. Any scheme that ADDS model calls to buy latency raises total tokens without raising the rate â€” and can **net-slow** a quota-bound run. (Anthropic's \"15Ã— tokens for 90% faster\" is a *metered-API* result; it inverts on a fixed-quota subscription.)\n17\t\n18\t## WHAT ACTUALLY DELAYS A DELIVERABLE (and why the build loop isn't it)\n19\t- **No wall-clock data exists to optimize.** Execution logs record only dates + fix-iteration counts; dashboard `elapsed`/`token%` are hardcoded `null`. 54 waves (v4â€“v11) were essentially same-day. STEP ZERO before building anything: instrument per-phase elapsed/tokens (`transport.mjs` already logs `duration_ms`/`output_tokens` per call; nothing aggregates them).\n20\t- **Calendar time is dominated by HUMAN approval gates, not machine time.** Crucible has 3 standing you-approve HALTs; Anchor layers per-UI-wave \"John signs off\" gates; v6â€“v11 builds sat on branches for days awaiting sign-off. Foreman itself auto-advances with zero scheduled human gates. *(Single-origin finding; well-grounded in dated STATUS lines; worth measuring.)*\n21\t\n22\t## RECOMMENDATIONS (ranked, risk-rated)\n23\t\n24\t| # | Lever | Verdict | Wall-clock | Robustness | Tokens |\n25\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n26\t| 1 | **Concurrent read-only reviewers** (`forâ€¦await` â†’ `Promise.allSettled`, wave-engine.mjs:861) | **SHIP** | ~1.2â€“1.3Ã— *tail only* | preserved (no commits/checkpoint change; merge is order-independent by stable-id, collectFindings:678-694) | still RÃ— (both reviewers draw the window) |\n27\t| 2 | **Reword the topology rule** â†’ *\"No parallel agent may MUTATE a shared, under-specified decision surface\"*; keep it an unconditional bright line | **SHIP** | n/a (accuracy) | strengthens it | none |\n28\t| 3 | **Gate âˆ¥ review overlap** (start read-only reviewers while the off-quota `spawnSync` test gate runs) | **MEASURE then maybe** | overlaps off-quota CPU with on-quota model â€” additive IF gate is slow vs review | no commits / no model-call increase | none added |\n29\t| 4 | **Reduce mean fix-iterations *k*** (better EXECUTE prompt / stronger model for EXECUTE only) | **PURSUE (non-parallel)** | ~33% (k 2â†’1); multiplier on the whole cycle | preserved | **fewer** tokens |\n30\t| 5 | **Cheaper/faster SECOND reviewer model** (see Open Questions) | **PURSUE â€” with guards** | ~17â€“22% | preserved | **halves** review tokens |\n31\t| 6 | **Parallelism across *independent projects*** (separate plans/repos/checkpoints) | **VALID, different goal** | real throughput win, zero merge risk | preserved (`worktrees.py` per-session isolation already fits) | linear in projects |\n32\t| 7 | **Wave-DAG / worktree-band parallelism** | **DO NOT SHIP (default)** | ~1.2â€“1.6Ã— only on the minority of plans with independent bands, after merge+re-gate tax | N commit chains forfeit the single-checkpoint/single-branch resume guarantee | higher |\n33\t| 8 | **Speculative/parallel EXECUTE, speculative fixes, cross-wave pipelining** | **REJECT** | small/illusory | violates ascending truth-gate | ~2Ã— â€” anti-lever when quota-bound |\n34\t| 9 | **Shrink the gate of record to \"impacted tests only\"** | **REJECT** | â€” | IS the vacuous-GREEN attack the full-suite gate exists to close | â€” |\n35\t\n36\t**Adjudicated conflict:** an affected-tests pre-check is acceptable ONLY as an inner-loop fast-feedback accelerator â€” the **gate of record stays the full suite** (anti-reward-hacking / vacuous-GREEN invariant).\n37\t\n38\t## WHY PARALLEL-WRITE BRANCHES FAIL (OBSERVED)\n39\t- Disjoint-write-set is an *output* of execution, not a pre-gate â€” collisions are only knowable after running N agents.\n40\t- The conflict is semantic, not file-level (one wave adds `grass_origin=\"\"`, another assumes `None`).\n41\t- The gate is a boolean, not a ranker â€” fan-out needs a ranker to pick among test-passing candidates; Foreman has only GO/NO-GO, so fan-out silently commits design drift.\n42\t- No merge machinery exists (`grep worktree bin/` = nothing); one linear branch, per-file staging, single-`last_commit` reconcile that HALTs if HEAD is >1 commit ahead. Worktree-bands forfeit the durability that makes Foreman trustworthy.\n43\t- R4 (\"declare bands in Crucible\") is under-costed: Crucible's `dependsOn` is `{type:['string','null']}` (single linear predecessor, no DAG/file-set), and zero real plans even populate it.\n44\t\n45\t## CITATION INTEGRITY\n46\t\"Anthropic + Cognition both agree\" = 2 genuinely independent origins (opposite priors, no cross-citation) â€” not a bandwagon. BUT neither has OBSERVED parallel-code-writing data; that sub-claim is CORROBORATED-by-opinion, not OBSERVED. SKILL.md over-generalizes by citing Anthropic (whose own system parallelizes read-only research for a 90% time cut) as an anti-parallel witness; the well-supported claim is the narrower shared-mutation one (= recommendation #2).\n47\t\n48\t## OPEN QUESTIONS FOR PLANNING (John's concerns about the cheaper-reviewer lever #5)\n49\t\n50\tThese convert #5 from \"swap in a cheap model\" into a guarded design. **Planning should treat these as locked requirements, not options.**\n51\t\n52\t### Q1 â€” \"How do we know a lower-tier reviewer is good enough? We might miss something.\"\n53\t- **The reviewer is NOT the arbiter of correctness â€” the orchestrator-run full-suite test GATE is.** Reviewers are an adversarial *supplement* that catches what tests miss (design drift, missing tests, semantic issues). A weaker reviewer degrades the supplement, not the correctness floor. Tests-pass is unchanged.\n54\t- **Keep ONE reviewer strong; make only the SECOND one cheap.** Because a BLOCKER requires â‰¥2 agreement, a cheap second reviewer can only *fail to confirm* a real blocker (a miss) â€” it can never raise a false blocker alone. You lose a blocker only if BOTH miss. This structurally bounds the downside to \"strong model raised it, cheap model didn't confirm.\"\n55\t- **Gate the swap on a measured metric, not a vibe.** Shadow period: run cheap + strong reviewer together for N waves and compute **recall** = of the blockers the strong reviewer raised, what fraction did the cheap reviewer also raise? Also test against a held-out set of historical known-bad diffs / past blockers (false-negative rate). Promote the cheap tier only if recall â‰¥ a pre-registered threshold; else don't.\n56\t\n57\t### Q2 â€” \"Could it get STUCK on a low tier and never upgrade when a better model ships?\"\n58\t- **Never hard-code a model id.** Resolve the reviewer model from a single config var pointing at a model **tier/policy** (\"reviewer = current cheap-tier\"), resolved at run time â€” same discipline Foreman already uses for `REVIEWER_COUNT`. Pin by *role/tier*, not by version string.\n59\t- **Staleness guard + scheduled re-calibration.** A dated re-calibration job (or a healthcheck check) flags \"reviewer model last calibrated > X days\" or \"a newer model in this tier exists,\" and re-runs the Q1 recall calibration on a model-release trigger so the tier can move up. This is a natural `/schedule` or healthcheck owner.\n60\t- **Net:** the swap is safe only as a *policy with a measured promotion gate and a scheduled re-evaluation*, never a static model pin.\n61\t\n62\t## NET\n63\tForeman should stay single-threaded for ALL write work. Ship concurrent reviewers (latency-tail win) + the reword. Treat the cheap-second-reviewer as a guarded, measured, tier-resolved policy (Q1/Q2). The biggest real wins for \"time to deliverable\" are non-parallel: instrument first, reduce fix-iterations, overlap the off-quota gate with review, and â€” for more deliverables/day â€” run independent projects in parallel.\n64\t\n65\t## SOURCES\n66\t- Anthropic â€” How we built our multi-agent research system: https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/multi-agent-research-system\n67\t- Cognition â€” Don't Build Multi-Agents: https://cognition.com/blog/dont-build-multi-agents\n68\t- OBSERVED Foreman/Crucible source (`C:\\dev\\trio\\â€¦`); real `planning/rnd-v*` plans; prior study `researchPrime/runs/foreman-review-2026-06-19/FINAL-REPORT.md`.\n69\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5047ed5e-8973-4065-9603-0887d640e5e4","uuid":"cb37514e-577c-4eb1-85f6-97d4e2e4ba27","timestamp":"2026-06-23T00:48:27.809Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\dev\\Skill Foundry\\report.md","content":"# researchPrime â€” Can Foreman run its build efforts in parallel to cut wall-clock?\n\n> Research session on grass idea **grass-4199 \"Improved Foreman (make parallel)\"** (Skill Foundry).\n> Goal: reduce wall-clock to a Foreman deliverable WITHOUT reducing structural integrity, effectiveness, or robustness.\n\n## STAMPS (honesty)\n- **Mode:** ENGINE-capable (Node import probe GO; 28 trio symbols crossed). Phase-3 governed loop run via **real fresh-context sub-agents** applying the engine's governance rules (heterogeneous reviewers, â‰¥2-agree for a blocker, a separate context-free Judge, convergence-until-dry) â€” NOT the Node engine's in-process scoring path.\n- **ISOLATION:** real (parallel-swarm host; each reviewer a genuinely separate model context).\n- **cross_model: false** â€” single model substrate. Correlated-blind-spot caveat: the 3 reviewers share a model family. Mitigation: core findings are grounded in OBSERVED Foreman/Crucible source AND independently corroborated by the separate prior study `researchPrime/runs/foreman-review-2026-06-19/FINAL-REPORT.md`.\n- **Evidence ladder:** OBSERVED (code) > CORROBORATED > CLAIMED > UNVERIFIED.\n\n## BOTTOM LINE\nParallelism is **mostly the wrong lever** for the stated goal. The \"don't parallelize code-writing\" rule is correct; the boundary is sharper than the one-line rule states. Two independent reasons make write-parallelism lose (it is \"over-determined\"):\n\n1. **Conflict-safety.** Parallel writers conflict on **semantic interface contracts**, not just files. In real plans nearly every wave threads a shared record field / seed builder through ~4 files at once; a single sequential author IS the guarantee. A file-diff \"disjointness\" check is blind to the semantic collision.\n2. **Quota topology (decisive).** Every model step â€” coder OR reviewer â€” draws from ONE Pro/Max subscription window (~1â€“3 waves/window; no live quota API). N parallel model agents drain the one window NÃ— faster, then stall. So `wall_clock_floor â‰ˆ total_tokens / window_rate`. Any scheme that ADDS model calls to buy latency raises total tokens without raising the rate â€” and can **net-slow** a quota-bound run. (Anthropic's \"15Ã— tokens for 90% faster\" is a *metered-API* result; it inverts on a fixed-quota subscription.)\n\n## WHAT ACTUALLY DELAYS A DELIVERABLE (and why the build loop isn't it)\n- **No wall-clock data exists to optimize.** Execution logs record only dates + fix-iteration counts; dashboard `elapsed`/`token%` are hardcoded `null`. 54 waves (v4â€“v11) were essentially same-day. STEP ZERO before building anything: instrument per-phase elapsed/tokens (`transport.mjs` already logs `duration_ms`/`output_tokens` per call; nothing aggregates them).\n- **Calendar time is dominated by HUMAN approval gates, not machine time.** Crucible has 3 standing you-approve HALTs; Anchor layers per-UI-wave \"John signs off\" gates; v6â€“v11 builds sat on branches for days awaiting sign-off. Foreman itself auto-advances with zero scheduled human gates. *(Single-origin finding; well-grounded in dated STATUS lines; worth measuring.)*\n\n## RECOMMENDATIONS (ranked, risk-rated)\n\n| # | Lever | Verdict | Wall-clock | Robustness | Tokens |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **Concurrent read-only reviewers** (`forâ€¦await` â†’ `Promise.allSettled`, wave-engine.mjs:861) | **SHIP** | ~1.2â€“1.3Ã— *tail only* | preserved (no commits/checkpoint change; merge is order-independent by stable-id, collectFindings:678-694) | still RÃ— (both reviewers draw the window) |\n| 2 | **Reword the topology rule** â†’ *\"No parallel agent may MUTATE a shared, under-specified decision surface\"*; keep it an unconditional bright line | **SHIP** | n/a (accuracy) | strengthens it | none |\n| 3 | **Gate âˆ¥ review overlap** (start read-only reviewers while the off-quota `spawnSync` test gate runs) | **MEASURE then maybe** | overlaps off-quota CPU with on-quota model â€” additive IF gate is slow vs review | no commits / no model-call increase | none added |\n| 4 | **Reduce mean fix-iterations *k*** (better EXECUTE prompt / stronger model for EXECUTE only) | **PURSUE (non-parallel)** | ~33% (k 2â†’1); multiplier on the whole cycle | preserved | **fewer** tokens |\n| 5 | **Cheaper/faster SECOND reviewer model** (see Open Questions) | **PURSUE â€” with guards** | ~17â€“22% | preserved | **halves** review tokens |\n| 6 | **Parallelism across *independent projects*** (separate plans/repos/checkpoints) | **VALID, different goal** | real throughput win, zero merge risk | preserved (`worktrees.py` per-session isolation already fits) | linear in projects |\n| 7 | **Wave-DAG / worktree-band parallelism** | **DO NOT SHIP (default)** | ~1.2â€“1.6Ã— only on the minority of plans with independent bands, after merge+re-gate tax | N commit chains forfeit the single-checkpoint/single-branch resume guarantee | higher |\n| 8 | **Speculative/parallel EXECUTE, speculative fixes, cross-wave pipelining** | **REJECT** | small/illusory | violates ascending truth-gate | ~2Ã— â€” anti-lever when quota-bound |\n| 9 | **Shrink the gate of record to \"impacted tests only\"** | **REJECT** | â€” | IS the vacuous-GREEN attack the full-suite gate exists to close | â€” |\n\n**Adjudicated conflict:** an affected-tests pre-check is acceptable ONLY as an inner-loop fast-feedback accelerator â€” the **gate of record stays the full suite** (anti-reward-hacking / vacuous-GREEN invariant).\n\n## WHY PARALLEL-WRITE BRANCHES FAIL (OBSERVED)\n- Disjoint-write-set is an *output* of execution, not a pre-gate â€” collisions are only knowable after running N agents.\n- The conflict is semantic, not file-level (one wave adds `grass_origin=\"\"`, another assumes `None`).\n- The gate is a boolean, not a ranker â€” fan-out needs a ranker to pick among test-passing candidates; Foreman has only GO/NO-GO, so fan-out silently commits design drift.\n- No merge machinery exists (`grep worktree bin/` = nothing); one linear branch, per-file staging, single-`last_commit` reconcile that HALTs if HEAD is >1 commit ahead. Worktree-bands forfeit the durability that makes Foreman trustworthy.\n- R4 (\"declare bands in Crucible\") is under-costed: Crucible's `dependsOn` is `{type:['string','null']}` (single linear predecessor, no DAG/file-set), and zero real plans even populate it.\n\n## CITATION INTEGRITY\n\"Anthropic + Cognition both agree\" = 2 genuinely independent origins (opposite priors, no cross-citation) â€” not a bandwagon. BUT neither has OBSERVED parallel-code-writing data; that sub-claim is CORROBORATED-by-opinion, not OBSERVED. SKILL.md over-generalizes by citing Anthropic (whose own system parallelizes read-only research for a 90% time cut) as an anti-parallel witness; the well-supported claim is the narrower shared-mutation one (= recommendation #2).\n\n## OPEN QUESTIONS FOR PLANNING (John's concerns about the cheaper-reviewer lever #5)\n\nThese convert #5 from \"swap in a cheap model\" into a guarded design. **Planning should treat these as locked requirements, not options.**\n\n### Q1 â€” \"How do we know a lower-tier reviewer is good enough? We might miss something.\"\n- **The reviewer is NOT the arbiter of correctness â€” the orchestrator-run full-suite test GATE is.** Reviewers are an adversarial *supplement* that catches what tests miss (design drift, missing tests, semantic issues). A weaker reviewer degrades the supplement, not the correctness floor. Tests-pass is unchanged.\n- **Keep ONE reviewer strong; make only the SECOND one cheap.** Because a BLOCKER requires â‰¥2 agreement, a cheap second reviewer can only *fail to confirm* a real blocker (a miss) â€” it can never raise a false blocker alone. You lose a blocker only if BOTH miss. This structurally bounds the downside to \"strong model raised it, cheap model didn't confirm.\"\n- **Gate the swap on a measured metric, not a vibe.** Shadow period: run cheap + strong reviewer together for N waves and compute **recall** = of the blockers the strong reviewer raised, what fraction did the cheap reviewer also raise? Also test against a held-out set of historical known-bad diffs / past blockers (false-negative rate). Promote the cheap tier only if recall â‰¥ a pre-registered threshold; else don't.\n\n### Q2 â€” \"Could it get STUCK on a low tier and never upgrade when a better model ships?\"\n- **Never hard-code a model id.** Resolve the reviewer model from a single config var pointing at a model **tier/policy** (\"reviewer = current cheap-tier\"), resolved at run time â€” same discipline Foreman already uses for `REVIEWER_COUNT`. Pin by *role/tier*, not by version string.\n- **Staleness guard + scheduled re-calibration.** A dated re-calibration job (or a healthcheck check) flags \"reviewer model last calibrated > X days\" or \"a newer model in this tier exists,\" and re-runs the Q1 recall calibration on a model-release trigger so the tier can move up. This is a natural `/schedule` or healthcheck owner.\n- **Net:** the swap is safe only as a *policy with a measured promotion gate and a scheduled re-evaluation*, never a static model pin.\n\n## NET\nForeman should stay single-threaded for ALL write work. Ship concurrent reviewers (latency-tail win) + the reword. Treat the cheap-second-reviewer as a guarded, measured, tier-resolved policy (Q1/Q2). The biggest real wins for \"time to deliverable\" are non-parallel: instrument first, reduce fix-iterations, overlap the off-quota gate with review, and â€” for more deliverables/day â€” run independent projects in parallel.\n\n## SOURCES\n- Anthropic â€” How we built our multi-agent research system: https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/multi-agent-research-system\n- Cognition â€” Don't Build Multi-Agents: https://cognition.com/blog/dont-build-multi-agents\n- OBSERVED Foreman/Crucible source (`C:\\dev\\trio\\â€¦`); real `planning/rnd-v*` plans; prior study `researchPrime/runs/foreman-review-2026-06-19/FINAL-REPORT.md`.\n","numLines":69,"startLine":1,"totalLines":69}}}
